# Configuration reference for pco-mcp — NOT a .env file, and not loaded by the server.
#
# The server reads two JSON files from your home directory plus a few environment
# variables. Do not put real credentials in this repository.
#
# Reads and controlled writes against Planning Center. The eleven existing tools issue
# only HTTP GET to PCO. The nine write tools (plans, plan items, plan times) issue
# POST/PATCH/DELETE, only through pco_writes.py, only for an explicitly named account,
# and only when confirm=True: confirm=False returns a preview and writes nothing. Writes
# are never retried automatically and POST is never replayed; 5xx/transport failures
# report outcome_unknown=true. The Personal Access Token needs write permission for
# writes; a read-only token fails with 403 on confirm=True.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. ~/.mpco_credentials.json — one entry per account (secrets live ONLY here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each top-level key is an account name; `account=` on every read tool selects one for
# that call, and pco_use_account selects one for the current chat. Write tools require
# `account=` to be one of these concrete names: `account="all"`, an omitted account, a
# pco_use_account selection, and the installation default are rejected for writes.
# Every write result is tagged with account, org_name, account_source="explicit".
# Credentials are a Planning Center Personal Access Token: appId + secret.
# `orgName` is optional and is what tools report as `org_name`.
#
# {
#   "main": {
#     "appId": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
#     "appSecret": "pco_pat_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
#     "orgName": "Main Campus"
#   },
#   "north": {
#     "appId": "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy",
#     "appSecret": "pco_pat_yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy",
#     "orgName": "North Campus"
#   }
# }
#
# ~/.mpco_config.json — non-secret preferences
# Written only by pco_set_default_account(..., persist=True). A per-chat
# pco_use_account selection lives in a small non-secret runtime file under
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pco-mcp/ (or ~/.cache/pco-mcp/), never in this config.
#
# {
#   "defaultAccount": "north",
#   "orgNames": { "main": "Main Campus" }
# }
#
# org_name precedence: creds-entry "orgName"/"org_name" > config "orgNames"[account]
# > account name.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Environment variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT
#   The INSTALLATION default, chosen when a read tool call omits `account` and
#   this chat has no selection. Writes never use it; they require an explicit
#   concrete account name. It is computed once per server process and kept as a
#   snapshot; editing this variable or ~/.mpco_config.json later does not change
#   a running process, only new chats. Wins over ~/.mpco_config.json and over
#   the first key in ~/.mpco_credentials.json.
#
#   Per-chat override: call pco_use_account("north") in that chat. It writes only
#   a small non-secret runtime file keyed to that chat's lazy-tool process and
#   does not affect other chats. pco_use_account() clears it.
#   To change the installation default for future chats:
#     pco_set_default_account("north", persist=True)  -> writes ~/.mpco_config.json
#   pco_set_default_account("north") without persist changes only the current chat.
#
# Default resolution order (snapshotted per process):
#   PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT
#   > ~/.mpco_config.json "defaultAccount"
#   > first key in ~/.mpco_credentials.json
#   > "env" (when env credentials are set)
#   > "main"
#
# PCO_APP_ID + PCO_SECRET (or PCO_APP_SECRET)
#   Legacy env-only credentials, exposed as a synthetic account named "env".
#   When a credentials file exists, its named entries always win and env creds
#   are used only for `account="env"`. When no credentials file exists (or it
#   has no accounts), the env pair serves any requested account name for reads,
#   so existing single-account setups keep working unchanged. Writes are
#   stricter: in that env-only mode the only configured write name is `env`;
#   read aliases such as "main" are not accepted as write targets.
#
# PCO_SECRET and PCO_APP_SECRET are equivalent; PCO_SECRET is read first.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. account selection per call (read tools)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Precedence for read tool calls:
#   1. explicit `account=` argument (name or "all") — always wins
#   2. this chat's selection set by pco_use_account — per-chat runtime file
#      keyed to the stable lazy-tool process; no config write
#   3. installation default snapshot (PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT / config / creds order)
#
#   account omitted -> chat selection, else the installation default snapshot
#   account="north"    -> that account; an unknown name raises
#   account="all"   -> fan out over every configured account, ignoring the chat
#                      selection; returns
#                      {"account": "all", "account_source": "all",
#                       "accounts": [...],
#                       "results": [{"account", "org_name", "data"}],
#                       "errors": [{"account", "org_name", "error"}]}
#                      Partial failures stay in `errors`; all failures raise.
#
# Single-account results carry "account_source": "explicit" | "chat" | "installation".
# Each chat has its own lazy-tool process, so selections never leak between chats
# or parallel agents; pco_account_status() shows this chat's routing and scope_id.
#
# Writes do NOT use this precedence. The nine write tools (pco_create_plan,
# pco_update_plan, pco_delete_plan, pco_create_plan_item, pco_update_plan_item,
# pco_delete_plan_item, pco_create_plan_time, pco_update_plan_time,
# pco_delete_plan_time) require `account=` to be a concrete configured name.
# account="all", an omitted account, a pco_use_account selection, and the
# installation default are rejected; there is no fallback.
#
# Preview then confirm:
#   confirm=False (default) -> read-only preflight, then
#     {"mode": "preview", "would_write": false, "confirm_required": true,
#      "account", "org_name", "account_source": "explicit", "operation",
#      "method", "path", "payload", "preflight", "message"}
#     Nothing is mutated.
#   confirm=True -> exactly one POST/PATCH/DELETE; success returns
#     {"mode": "write", "written": true, "account", "org_name",
#      "account_source": "explicit", "operation", "method", "path",
#      "status_code", "request_id", "resource", "deleted", "message"}
#
#   pco_create_plan(account="north", service_type_id="123456", title="Christmas Eve")               # preview
#   pco_create_plan(account="north", service_type_id="123456", title="Christmas Eve", confirm=True) # write
#
# Deletes require an exact guard, compared before mutating:
#   pco_delete_plan       expected_title and/or expected_sort_date (at least one;
#                         sort_date alone covers plans with a null title)
#   pco_delete_plan_item  expected_title
#   pco_delete_plan_time  expected_starts_at
# pco_update_plan accepts expected_title and/or expected_sort_date; plan deletes
# also accept expected_sort_date (date-only YYYY-MM-DD or a full instant).
# pco_update_plan_item accepts expected_title; pco_update_plan_time accepts
# expected_starts_at. All are optional guards except a delete plan requires at
# least one of expected_title/expected_sort_date.
#
# No automatic retries: 429/5xx/timeouts are not retried and POST is never
# replayed. 429 -> wait and re-preview. 5xx/transport -> outcome_unknown=true;
# verify in Planning Center before retrying. Other 4xx -> WriteError; 403 means
# the token lacks write permission.
#
# Write paths are allowlisted to numeric ids under:
#   /service_types/{st}/plans                          POST
#   /service_types/{st}/plans/{plan}                   PATCH, DELETE
#   /service_types/{st}/plans/{plan}/items             POST
#   /service_types/{st}/plans/{plan}/items/{item}      PATCH, DELETE
#   /service_types/{st}/plans/{plan}/plan_times        POST
#   /service_types/{st}/plan_times/{time}              PATCH, DELETE
